  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1243 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nThe cold front pushes south of the Red River by Tuesday morning,\nushering in modest cold air advection and northerly surface flow.\nDESI interquartile temperature spreads highlight high confidence\nin near-normal conditions across northern and central OK on\nTuesday and Wednesday (highs 88-94\u00b0F; >80% probability Tmax\n<95\u00b0F). It is August, so even normal temps are on the hot side.\nFurther south near the Red River, the upper level ridge axis will\nkeep temperatures in the upper 90s to near 100\u00b0F (60% chance Tmax\n>=98\u00b0F). Boundary layer drying behind the front will keep relative\nhumidity lower, tempering overall heat index values area- wide\nthrough midweek. Yet, this is merely a temporary thing. \n\nNear normal temperatures will...you guessed it...get back to the\ntriple digits over the weekend as the subtropical ridge\nreannounces its presence with authority over the southern plains.\nIn the extended period, there are only two scenarios that may \nplay out. First, the ridge dominates and strengthens to near 600 \ndam and anchors over Oklahoma and Texas. The EPS/GEFS vast \nmajority shows this with about 80% confidence. This would mean \nconvection is completely suppressed and internal fights over the \nthermostat continues. The second scenario is the ridge stalling \nover New Mexico, keeping northwest flow across Oklahoma, and \ngiving some hope for a nocturnal MCS. However, This scenario is on\nthe lower end of things, giving about a 20% probability. \n\nEnsemble cluster analysis overwhelmingly favors Scenario A (80%),\nshowing rapid 500mb height rises and warm 850mb thermal advection\nreturning across the Southern Plains by Friday afternoon. \nGuidance and NBM exceedance probabilities for Tmax >=100\u00b0F climb \nback above 75% across most of Oklahoma and Arkansas by Saturday. \n\nTreat the midweek frontal passage as a temporary break, preparing\nfor high-confidence returns to excessive heat headline thresholds\nby the weekend. Subsequent cycles will monitor for any ensemble \ntrends toward the cooler, more active Scenario B (20%).",
